Most tree questions share one recursive shape: get the answer for the left subtree, get it for the right, and combine both with the current node. The base case is nil, and for counts and sums it returns 0.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

type TreeNode struct {
	Val         int
	Left, Right *TreeNode
}

{{code}}

func main() {
	root := &TreeNode{
		Val:   1,
		Left:  &TreeNode{Val: 2, Left: &TreeNode{Val: 4}, Right: &TreeNode{Val: 5}},
		Right: &TreeNode{Val: 3, Left: &TreeNode{Val: 6}, Right: &TreeNode{Val: 7}},
	}
	fmt.Println("nodes:", countNodes(root))
}
```

```go
func countNodes(n *TreeNode) int {
	if n == nil {
		return 0
	}
	return 1 + countNodes(n.Left) + countNodes(n.Right)
}
```

The **height** is the number of nodes on the longest path down from the root: 1 plus the taller subtree's height. Go 1.16 has no built-in `max`, so write a `maxInt` helper. A **leaf** is a node whose `Left` and `Right` are both nil. To count leaves, return 1 for a leaf and the sum of both subtrees for any other node.

When 0 is a wrong answer for nil, return a neutral value instead. For a maximum, nil returns `math.MinInt64`, so any real value beats it. To find even values test `v%2 == 0`. The test `v%2 == 1` misses negative odd values, because `-3 % 2` is `-1` in Go.

Only one path from the root is active at a time, so the call stack grows with the height, not the node count.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

type TreeNode struct {
	Val         int
	Left, Right *TreeNode
}

func maxInt(a, b int) int {
	if a > b {
		return a
	}
	return b
}

func height(n *TreeNode) int {
	if n == nil {
		return 0
	}
	return 1 + maxInt(height(n.Left), height(n.Right))
}

func countNodes(n *TreeNode) int {
	if n == nil {
		return 0
	}
	return 1 + countNodes(n.Left) + countNodes(n.Right)
}

func sumValues(n *TreeNode) int {
	if n == nil {
		return 0
	}
	return n.Val + sumValues(n.Left) + sumValues(n.Right)
}

func countLeaves(n *TreeNode) int {
	if n == nil {
		return 0
	}
	if n.Left == nil && n.Right == nil {
		return 1
	}
	return countLeaves(n.Left) + countLeaves(n.Right)
}

func main() {
	root := &TreeNode{
		Val:   1,
		Left:  &TreeNode{Val: 2, Left: &TreeNode{Val: 4}, Right: &TreeNode{Val: 5}},
		Right: &TreeNode{Val: 3, Left: &TreeNode{Val: 6}, Right: &TreeNode{Val: 7}},
	}
	fmt.Println("height:", height(root))
	fmt.Println("nodes:", countNodes(root))
	fmt.Println("sum:", sumValues(root))
	fmt.Println("leaves:", countLeaves(root))
}

